Looking at the DEFINITION, the key discriminating property is that the score gives "the fraction of items that have become **universally solvable**" — i.e., pass-everything items only. An item that fails-everything is *not* universally solvable; it is universally *unsolvable*. A metric that counts both "pass-everything **or** fail-everything" is a discrimination/separation metric, not a universal-solvability (exhaustion) score.

